From 3891b25ec838daa588aa8d24e697d81acfddc55c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 01:30:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: wake every conduit TX queue on EN751221
 completion

tag_mtk stores the DSA port index in skb->queue_mapping, so skb_tx_hash()
takes it as a recorded RX queue and every user port ends up on its own
conduit TX queue (port 2 -> tx-1, port 3 -> tx-2, ...).  All of them feed
the single generation-1 hardware ring.  econet_qdma_xmit_mips() returns
-EBUSY before posting the skb when the free list is exhausted and the
conduit xmit then stops that netdev queue; econet_poll_tx_complete() only
woke the queue of the skb it had just freed.  A queue stopped on ring-full
owns no descriptor, so no later completion ever carries its mapping and it
stays DRV_XOFF forever while the ring drains through the other queues: the
port silently stops transmitting (RX keeps working, nothing is logged, no
counter is incremented) until the conduit is re-initialised.

On a TP-Link Archer XR500v (EN7526G) this killed the port carrying the home
LAN after a few hours whenever a download burst to another port filled the
ring.  ip link set eth0 down/up (which restarts every conduit queue) brought
it back immediately without touching the switches.

Do what airoha_qdma_wake_netdev_txqs() already does for generation 2:
remember the netdev of any freed skb and wake all of its TX queues once
after the drain loop.  netif_tx_wake_all_queues() only clears DRV_XOFF, so
BQL throttling is preserved; if the ring fills again the next xmit re-stops
the queue and the next completion re-wakes it.

Signed-off-by: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
(cherry picked from commit 0519ae66a35ca29d37e0c585288502e5b84c30e5)
Signed-off-by: Matheus Sampaio Queiroga <srherobrine20@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
index a6a08b4e2..443259b58 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -890,6 +890,7 @@ static int econet_poll_tx_complete(struct napi_struct *napi, int budget)
 {
 	struct qregs_doneq_state state;
 	struct econet_tx_doneq *done_q;
+	struct net_device *wake_dev = NULL;
 	struct airoha_qdma_mips *qdma;
 	int irq_queued;
 	u32 done = 0, head;
@@ -959,12 +960,30 @@ static int econet_poll_tx_complete(struct napi_struct *napi, int budget)
 		txq = netdev_get_tx_queue(skb->dev,
 					  skb_get_queue_mapping(skb));
 		netdev_tx_completed_queue(txq, 1, skb->len);
-		if (netif_tx_queue_stopped(txq))
-			netif_tx_wake_queue(txq);
+		/* Every net_device TX queue of this conduit (one per DSA user
+		 * port through tag_mtk's queue_mapping, hashed for the rest)
+		 * funnels into the same hardware ring, so the gating resource
+		 * is shared.  A queue stopped on ring-full holds no descriptor
+		 * of its own (econet_qdma_xmit_mips() returns -EBUSY before
+		 * posting the skb), so it can never produce a completion that
+		 * would wake it: waking only the queue of the skb just freed
+		 * strands it forever.  Defer a device-wide wake to after the
+		 * drain loop instead.
+		 */
+		wake_dev = skb->dev;
 
 		dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
 	}
 
+	/* Freed at least one ring slot: wake every TX queue of the conduit so
+	 * a descriptor-less stranded queue restarts.  netif_tx_wake_all_queues()
+	 * only clears DRV_XOFF, not BQL's STACK_XOFF, so BQL throttling is
+	 * preserved; if the ring fills up again the next xmit re-stops the queue
+	 * and the next completion re-wakes it.
+	 */
+	if (wake_dev)
+		netif_tx_wake_all_queues(wake_dev);
+
 	if (done) {
 		int i, len = done >> 7;
 
-- 
2.55.0

